  - [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m access throws more often than people expect: private browsing, storage disabled by policy, quota
    exhaustion, sandboxed iframes. Every read and write is wrapped.
  - On storage failure, [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m degrades to a module-scoped in-memory ID minted once per page load. The app keeps
    working and correlation narrows to a single page visit. One [38;5;153mconsole.warn[39m, emitted once rather than per call, so the
    degradation is visible without flooding the console.
  - A stored value is validated as a non-empty string before use, not trusted. [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m is user-writable, so
    "there's something there" isn't the same as "it's an ID." Anything malformed is treated as absent and re-minted.
  - If generation itself fails, [38;5;153mgetUserId()[39m returns [38;5;153mnull[39m and [38;5;153mlogin[39m proceeds with [38;5;153muserId: null[39m. It never throws into the
    submit handler.
  - [38;5;153mlogin[39m does not validate [38;5;153muserId[39m — it's a passthrough for logging only. I'll state explicitly in the spec that this
    value must never gate an authorization decision, since a client-minted, client-writable identifier is trivially
    forgeable. That restriction is what keeps this design safe; it's worth writing down rather than leaving to memory.

  [1mTesting.[0m [38;5;153mnode --test[39m via [38;5;153mnpm test[39m, zero dependencies, tests in [38;5;153mtest/tracking.test.js[39m. Covering: mint-on-empty, reuse
  on second call, re-mint on a corrupt stored value, the in-memory fallback when storage throws, and [38;5;153mlogin[39m both
  including [38;5;153muserId[39m and tolerating [38;5;153mnull[39m.

  One consequence you should agree to before I write the spec, because it touches [38;5;153mapp.js[39m beyond the signature change you
  asked for. Today [38;5;153mapp.js[39m calls [38;5;153mdocument.getElementById("login-form").addEventListener(...)[39m at top level, so the file
  cannot be loaded in Node at all — requiring it immediately throws on a missing DOM. To unit-test [38;5;153mlogin[39m, two small
  additions are needed: wrap the DOM wiring in [38;5;153mif (typeof document !== "undefined")[39m, and add a [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m tail
  guarded by [38;5;153mtypeof module !== "undefined"[39m. Both are inert in the browser — behavior there is unchanged — but they are
  edits beyond a parameter, so I'd rather you say yes than find them in a diff.
